stationary-phase-classical-limit-2f9a2f2.limits_and_boundary:
As \(\hbar\) approaches zero (the classical limit), the phase \(S/\hbar\) becomes very large for any variation in the path. This causes the integrand \(e^{iS/\hbar}\) to oscillate rapidly, leading to destructive interference among most paths. Only paths where the action is stationary (
$$ \delta S = 0 $$
), i.e., the classical paths, maintain constructive interference and dominate the integral. If \(S\) were a constant (which is not generally true for a path integral), the integral would diverge. However, by considering paths in the neighborhood of a stationary point, the integral can be evaluated to yield the exponential of the stationary action.

